::PC_U01_L05_Q01::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.11
Question:
What is the inverse of f(x) = x - 4
{
~B. f(x) \= x - 4#This repeats the original function instead of reversing it.
~C. f(x) \= 4x#Multiplication by 4 does not undo subtracting 4.
~D. f(x) \= x / 4#Division by 4 would undo multiplication by 4, not subtraction.
=A. f(x) \= x + 4#Correct. Adding 4 undoes subtracting 4.
}

::PC_U01_L05_Q02::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.11
Question:
What is the inverse of f(x) = 3x²
{
~A. f(x) \= 3x#This repeats the original function.
=B. f(x) \= x / 3#Correct. Dividing by 3 undoes multiplying by 3.
~C. f(x) \= x - 3#Subtraction does not undo multiplication by 3.
~D. f(x) \= x + 3#Addition does not undo multiplication by 3.
}

::PC_U01_L05_Q03::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.11
Question:
If f(8) = -2 and f has an inverse, what is f(-2)
{
=D. 8#Correct. The inverse maps the output -2 back to the input 8.
~A. -16#This multiplies the values and does not represent the inverse.
~B. -2#This keeps the output instead of reversing the relationship.
~C. 6#This subtracts values but does not use inverse meaning.
}

::PC_U01_L05_Q04::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.11
Question:
What graph relationship do inverse functions have
{
~B. They are reflected across the x-axis.#Reflection across the x-axis changes output signs, not inverse relationships.
~C. They are reflected across the y-axis.#Reflection across the y-axis changes input signs, not inverse relationships.
=A. They are reflected across the line y \= x.#Correct. Inverses swap x and y coordinates.
~D. They are vertical shifts of each other.#Inverse functions are not identified by a vertical shift.
}

::PC_U01_L05_Q05::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.11
Question:
What is the inverse of f(x) = x / 5
{
=B. f(x) \= 5x#Correct. Multiplying by 5 undoes dividing by 5.
~A. f(x) \= x / 5#This repeats the original function.
~C. f(x) \= x + 5#Addition does not undo division by 5.
~D. f(x) \= x - 5#Subtraction does not undo division by 5.
}

::PC_U01_L05_Q06::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.11
Question:
What is the inverse of f(x) = 2x + 1
{
=B. f(x) \= (x - 1) / 2#Correct. Subtract 1, then divide by 2.
~A. f(x) \= (x + 1) / 2#This uses the wrong operation to undo +1.
~C. f(x) \= x / 2 + 1#This leaves the +1 instead of undoing it.
~D. f(x) \= 2x - 1#This does not undo the multiplication by 2.
}

::PC_U01_L05_Q07::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.12
Question:
Which restriction makes f(x) = x² one-to-one
{
~B. y >\= 0#This describes output values, not a domain restriction.
~C. all real numbers#Over all real numbers, negative and positive inputs can share the same output.
~D. x is not 0#Removing only 0 does not prevent pairs such as -2 and 2 from sharing an output.
=A. x >\= 0#Correct. Restricting to nonnegative inputs makes x² pass the horizontal line test.
}

::PC_U01_L05_Q08::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.11
Question:
If f(6) = 11, what must be true about f
{
=C. f(11) \= 6#Correct. The inverse statement means f maps 11 to 6.
~A. f(6) \= 11#This reverses the inverse relationship incorrectly.
~B. f(11) \= 11#The output should be 6, not 11.
~D. f(6) \= -11#There is no sign change implied by inverse notation.
}

::PC_U01_L05_Q09::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.11
Question:
What is f(f(x)) for inverse functions f and f
{
~A. 0#That would describe additive opposites, not inverse functions.
~B. 1#That would describe reciprocal products, not function inverses.
~D. f(x)#The inverse should undo f, leaving x.
=C. x#Correct. A function composed with its inverse returns the original input.
}

::PC_U01_L05_Q10::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.11
Question:
What is the inverse of f(x) = x + 12
{
~B. f(x) \= 12x#Multiplication does not undo addition.
=A. f(x) \= x - 12#Correct. Subtracting 12 undoes adding 12.
~C. f(x) \= x / 12#Division does not undo addition.
~D. f(x) \= x + 12#This repeats the original function.
}

::PC_U01_L05_Q11::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.12
Question:
Which test helps determine whether a graph has an inverse function without restricting the domain
{
~A. Distance formula test#Distance formula does not determine invertibility.
~B. Vertical line test only#The vertical line test checks whether a relation is a function, not whether it is one-to-one.
=C. Horizontal line test#Correct. Passing the horizontal line test shows the function is one-to-one.
~D. Slope-intercept test#This is not a standard test for invertibility.
}

::PC_U01_L05_Q12::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.13
Question:
A function converts Celsius to Fahrenheit. What does the inverse convert
{
=C. Fahrenheit to Celsius#Correct. The inverse reverses Celsius-to-Fahrenheit conversion.
~A. Celsius to Celsius#The inverse reverses the original conversion.
~B. Fahrenheit to Fahrenheit#The inverse does not keep the same input and output unit.
~D. Celsius to Kelvin#This is a different temperature conversion.
}

::PC_U01_L05_Q13::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.11
Question:
Which ordered pair must appear on f if (2, 9) is on f
{
~B. (2, -9)#This changes the sign instead of switching coordinates.
=A. (9, 2)#Correct. Inverses switch input and output coordinates.
~C. (-2, 9)#This changes the sign of the input.
~D. (2, 9)#This repeats the original ordered pair rather than reversing it.
}

::PC_U01_L05_Q14::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.11
Question:
What is the inverse of f(x) = (x - 3) / 4
{
~B. f(x) \= (x + 3) / 4#This does not reverse both operations.
=A. f(x) \= 4x + 3#Correct. Multiply by 4, then add 3.
~C. f(x) \= x / 4 - 3#This repeats the same operation order.
~D. f(x) \= 4x - 3#This does not undo the subtraction correctly after multiplying.
}

::PC_U01_L05_Q15::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.12
Question:
Why is f(x) = |x| not one-to-one on all real numbers
{
~A. It is always decreasing.#The graph decreases then increases, so this description is inaccurate.
~B. It has no y-intercept.#It has a y-intercept at 0.
~D. It has no domain.#Its domain is all real numbers.
=C. Opposite inputs can produce the same output.#Correct. For example, f(-3) and f(3) both equal 3.
}

::PC_U01_L05_Q16::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.11
Question:
Which equation shows correct inverse verification for f(x) = x + 2 and g(x) = x - 2
{
~A. f(x) + g(x) \= x#Adding does not verify inverse relationships.
=B. f(g(x)) \= x#Correct. f(g(x)) = (x - 2) + 2 = x.
~C. f(x) - g(x) \= x#Subtraction does not show the functions undo each other.
~D. f(x)g(x) \= x#Multiplication is not inverse verification.
}

::PC_U01_L05_Q17::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.13
Question:
If a function maps hours worked to pay earned, what does the inverse map
{
~A. hours worked to hours worked#This does not reverse the relationship.
=B. pay earned to hours worked#Correct. The inverse reverses the original input-output relationship.
~C. pay earned to pay earned#This keeps the output unit unchanged.
~D. hours worked to dollars squared#This does not represent the inverse context.
}

::PC_U01_L05_Q18::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.11
Question:
What is the inverse of f(x) = 5x - 10
{
~A. f(x) \= (x - 10) / 5#This repeats the subtraction instead of undoing it.
~B. f(x) \= x / 5 - 10#This does not reverse the order of operations.
=C. f(x) \= (x + 10) / 5#Correct. Add 10, then divide by 5.
~D. f(x) \= 5x + 10#This does not undo multiplication by 5.
}

::PC_U01_L05_Q19::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.12
Question:
Which domain restriction can make f(x) = (x - 1) invertible
{
~A. y >\= 0#This is a range statement, not a domain restriction.
~C. all real numbers#The full parabola is not one-to-one.
~D. x is not 1#Removing the vertex does not remove all repeated outputs.
=B. x >\= 1#Correct. One side of the parabola is one-to-one.
}

::PC_U01_L05_Q20::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.11
Question:
If f and g are inverses, what is g(f(4))
{
~A. 0#Inverse composition returns the original input, not zero.
~C. 1#This confuses inverse functions with reciprocal products.
=B. 4#Correct. g(f(4)) returns the original input 4.
~D. f(4)#The outer inverse should undo f.
}

::PC_U01_L05_Q21::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.11
Question:
Which first step best supports a solution for Inverse Functions and Domain Restrictions
{
~A. Skip directly to an answer without naming the given information.#Skipping the setup often leads to using the wrong method.
=C. Identify the given representation, quantities, and restrictions before calculating.#Correct. A strong Precalculus solution begins by identifying the representation and restrictions so the method matches the task.
~B. Use any formula from a different unit.#A different unit may use different assumptions or tools.
~D. Base the answer only on how a graph or expression appears.#Appearance can be misleading unless it is supported by values or relationships.
}

::PC_U01_L05_Q22::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.11
Question:
What evidence should a student use when explaining Inverse Functions and Domain Restrictions
{
=D. Use labeled values, equations, graphs, or tables from the problem as evidence.#Correct. Evidence must come from the given mathematical representation, not from a guess or unrelated rule.
~A. Ignore labels and units because they are not part of the calculation.#Labels and units help determine what the quantities mean.
~B. Use a future topic even when the current lesson gives enough information.#The current lesson evidence should be used before reaching for later topics.
~C. Use the longest answer choice as evidence.#Answer length is not mathematical evidence.
}

::PC_U01_L05_Q23::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.11
Question:
Which response best shows mastery of Inverse Functions and Domain Restrictions
{
~A. Change the given values to make the computation easier.#Changing given values changes the problem.
~C. Give only a final number with no reasoning.#A final number alone does not show how the relationship was used.
~D. Copy vocabulary without applying it to the problem.#Vocabulary must be connected to the actual representation or calculation.
=B. Show the steps, connect them to the lesson relationship, and interpret the result.#Correct. Mastery requires both accurate work and an interpretation connected to the lesson relationship.
}

::PC_U01_L05_Q24::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.11
Question:
What should be checked before finalizing work involving Inverse Functions and Domain Restrictions
{
~A. Check only that the answer choice is short.#Length does not show correctness.
~B. Remove restrictions because they make the problem harder.#Restrictions are part of the mathematical meaning.
~C. Assume every representation has the same domain.#Different representations may show different restrictions.
=D. Check that notation, units, domain restrictions, and the final interpretation match the task.#Correct. These checks help confirm that the answer is mathematically valid for the stated task.
}

::PC_U01_L05_Q25::
Question ID: PC_U01_L05_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.11
Question:
Which error should be avoided when working with Inverse Functions and Domain Restrictions
{
~B. Writing the given information first.#Writing givens first is a helpful setup, not the error.
~C. Explaining why a theorem, identity, or model applies.#Explaining why a method applies strengthens the solution.
=A. Using an unrelated formula or visual pattern without checking the lesson conditions.#Correct. Appearance alone is not reliable; the method must match the conditions in the lesson.
~D. Checking the final answer in the context of the problem.#Checking in context is a good habit, not an error.}
